== D. a second lesson, recorded because it bit me here ==
   'setsid CMD &' gives $! = setsid's pid ONLY when setsid forks; when it execs, $! is the child.
   setsid forks iff the caller is already a process-group leader, so which one you get depends on the
   invocation context. In run_mutant.sh 'ps -o comm= -p $!' found ndtwin_kernel; in this step it found
   nothing while the kernel was very much alive and holding :8000 and :6343 (see section B).
   Same shape as COMMON-BRIEF section 7's sudo warning, different tool. I stopped it by exact pid found
   through 'ps -eo pid=,comm=' (never pkill/pgrep -f) and re-verified the ports:
